# Markdown round trips and streamed parses

Handle $ARGUMENTS. `check`, or no argument, runs the gate bound. `deep` runs `--inline=3 --part=round-trip` and `--blocks=3` and compares their failures with Findings. `lower` runs `--lower` after a fix removes a known finding. `case <identity>` runs `--case=<identity>` to print one case's full detail. `update` brings the verification in line with changed code, as Coverage record says.

**The property.** Two rules hold for every source up to the bound. A source that `editor.api.markdown.parse` accepts serializes, and the serialized Markdown parses to the same document. A streamed parse with `parseSlice` that continues a previous preview of the same source gives the result a fresh `parseSlice` of that prefix gives, at every chunking. `docs/vision/plate.md` says streaming continues the prior result with `previous`, and that the Markdown runtime owns reuse and invalidation so consumers keep no parse cache. Reuse that changed the result would break that promise.

**The harm.** A round trip that changes the document loses content each time a user saves and reopens Markdown, or each time an AI reply goes through the codec. A streamed preview that differs from a fresh parse shows the user a document that changes when the stream ends, or a final document that differs from the source. Each Markdown fix before this check, such as e1fb01295e, covered only the cases someone had already found.

## What it checks

`packages/platejs/scripts/markdown-round-trip.ts` runs the real codec of `createTestEditor` in `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/__tests__/createTestEditor.tsx`, with `BaseIndentPlugin` given the indent targets of `apps/www/src/registry/components/editor/indent-static.tsx`. That editor has the plugins the Markdown stress cases use, and its remark plugins are math, GFM, the two CJK-friendly plugins and emoji. A block that the parse indents keeps its indent as it does in the shipped kit. The script checks the TypeScript code itself, not a model of it.

1. **Inline contents.** Every string of up to `--inline` tokens from 22 tokens: `a`, a space, `*`, `**`, `_`, a backtick, `~~`, `[a](b)`, `<br/>`, `<u>`, `</u>`, a backslash, a newline, `$`, `!`, `#`, `<`, `&`, `https://a.b`, `[^1]`, `|` and `:+1:`.
2. **Block forms.** 21 forms: paragraph, ATX heading, setext heading, quote, bullet, ordered and task items, table, fenced and indented code, math, div, callout, link reference, link definition, footnote definition, a lazy list item, thematic break, bold, link and a trailing break. 15 forms take every inline content. The setext heading, lazy list item, thematic break, bold, link and trailing break are fixed sources. Eight more fixed sources hold a URL that cannot stay a bare URL or that the parse keeps as text. `https://a.b.\na` and `https://a.b)\na` put a bare URL before a line break, and `<https://a.b'> a`, `<https://a.b"> a` and `<https://a.b;> a` end a URL in a character that GFM trims from a bare URL. The link plugin refuses the URL of `<https://a.b]> a`, so the parse keeps it as text. The parse also keeps `<div>www.a.b </div>` and `<div>a@b.cd </div>` as text. Each must parse again as text, though GFM finds an autolink literal in it. They run alone and join no sequence, because as block forms they would almost double the 902 round-trip sequences. Seven more fixed sources hold emphasis whose reading depends on the CJK plugins. `这是**重点。**然后`, `日本語の**「強調」**です`, `这是~~删除。~~文字`, `这是*斜体。*文字` and `한국어 **강조.**다음` end bold, strike or italic in punctuation beside a CJK letter, which CommonMark alone keeps as text. `这是\*\*重点。\*\*然后` escapes its asterisks and `a**"b"**c` holds no CJK character, so both parse as text and must parse again as text. No inline token holds a CJK character, so these seven are fixed sources, and they run alone for the reason the URL sources do. Two more forms leave a tag open that a count of tag names reads as closed, `<callout>` with `</callout>` in a code span and `</callout> <callout>`. They join only the streamed sequences.
3. **Sequences.** Every list of up to `--blocks` forms, each holding `a`, joined by one or two newlines. The round trip runs 902 sequences at the gate and the stream 1,080.
4. **Spec examples.** Every example of CommonMark 0.31.2, read from the `commonmark-spec` package with each `→` as a tab, and the 41 examples of GFM 0.29.0.gfm.13 that CommonMark lacks, kept in `packages/platejs/scripts/markdown-round-trip-gfm.ts`. Each example runs the round trip at every bound. The `spec-stream` part also streams each one as `a\n\n<example>\n\na`, one character at a time. It continues the preview of each prefix to the next character, as a preview and as a strict parse, and compares each with a fresh parse of that prefix. A finding's detail starts with its example, such as `CommonMark 0.31.2 example 218`.
5. **Round trip.** Each form with each inline content, each sequence and each spec example goes through parse, serialize and parse again under the default policy. A parse that refuses the source counts in the summary's `refused` and is not a failure. The script compares documents after `nameDocumentListIds`, because each parse gives every list a new id. A source that holds a line ending is also parsed with each one written as CRLF and then as CR, and each parse must give the document of the LF form.
6. **Streaming.** For each sequence the script parses every prefix fresh, as a preview (`lossPolicy: 'allow', partial: true`) and as a strict parse. Then it continues each reachable preview to its own prefix and every longer one, as a preview and as a strict parse, and compares each with the fresh result. A continuation reads its previous result through the runtime's checkpoint. Every stored state is a preview of a prefix of the same source, so the checkpoint's editor, formats, limits, policy and source test are the same for all of them, and each segment's nodes, diagnostics and last root follow from the segment ends. So the script records each checkpoint, merges previews whose segment ends match, and still covers every chunking without walking each chain. A failed preview merges with a checkpoint that has no segments, because neither reuses anything. When a preview has no recorded checkpoint, or a segment has no whole-number end, the script exits 2, because it can no longer read `markdownCheckpoints` in `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/internal/markdownConversion.ts`.
7. **Rules.** A case records its first failure per rule:
   - `markdown-line-endings`: a source whose CRLF or CR line endings parse to another document than its LF line endings.
   - `markdown-round-trip`: a source whose parse, serialized and parsed again, gives another document.
   - `markdown-serialize`: a parsed document that does not serialize, or whose Markdown does not parse.
   - `markdown-stream`: a preview that continues an earlier preview and differs from a fresh preview.
   - `markdown-stream-final`: a strict parse that continues a preview and differs from a fresh strict parse.
   - `markdown-throws`: a parse or serialize that throws.
8. It compares the findings with `packages/platejs/scripts/markdown-round-trip-baseline.json` through `tooling/scripts/finding-baseline.mjs`. Every finding is filed under `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib`. An identity is the source alone, and the detail names the chunking, because which chain first reaches a state changes whenever the codec's segments do.

## Run it

Run every command from the repository root.

- `pnpm check:markdown-round-trip` checks the gate bound: two inline tokens and two blocks. It compares 10,879 cases: 9,106 round trips, 679 of them spec examples that parse, 1,080 streamed sequences and 693 streamed spec examples. On a machine whose load average stood near 130, it took 19 to 30 s and 120 to 132 CPU-s. It prints one JSON summary line, then each finding the baseline does not list. `pnpm check markdown-round-trip` runs the same step through the check runner, and Local CI runs it on next after each landing.
- `--inline` and `--blocks` raise one part of the bound, and `--part=round-trip`, `--part=stream` or `--part=spec-stream` runs one part. `--inline=3 --part=round-trip` compares 166,957 cases in 436 CPU-s on a loaded machine, and `--blocks=3` compares 96,415 cases. Its stream half, `--blocks=3 --part=stream`, compares 49,656 cases in 1,771 CPU-s on a loaded machine. A bound other than the gate's, `--part` or `--case` skips the baseline and exits 1 on any failure.
- `--case='<identity or part of one>'` prints the full diff and stack of each failure whose identity contains it, such as `--case='"<br/><br/>"'`.
- `--jobs=<n>` sets the number of worker processes, the machine's cores up to 16 by default. Findings do not depend on it.
- Exit 0 is a pass and exit 1 a finding. Exit 2 is a bad flag, a crashed worker or a checkpoint the script cannot read. At the gate the run also fails when it compares other than 10,879 cases, the `GATE_CASES` constant, so a change that makes parse refuse more sources cannot shrink the gate while the baseline still matches. Update the constant only with the reason the count moved.
- After a fix removes a known finding, the gate fails until you run `pnpm check:markdown-round-trip --lower`. The baseline never grows past the base commit's copy. `--init` seeds only a rule the baseline lacks, and `--lower` and `--init` write nothing when the case count misses `GATE_CASES`.

## Read a failure

A finding prints its rule, identity and the first line of its detail:

```text
markdown-round-trip "<br/><br/>"
  serialized "<br />\n<br />\n"; the parse again differs at .children.0.children.0.text: "\n\n\n", not "\n\n"

markdown-stream "1. a\na\n\n1. a"
  chunked at 1,9,10, the continued result differs at .slice.content.1.listId: "b", not "a"
```

The first is a known finding, two breaks in a row that come back as three line endings. The second is what the gate prints when `findMarkdownSegmentBoundaries` stops keeping a list open, as What a pass proves says. The preview of the first 9 characters ends in `1`, which is not a list marker yet. So the segment scanner commits a boundary after the lazy line, and the continuation splits one list in two. Run `--case` on the identity for the full documents.

Tell a real bug from a modeling error before you change code:

- **Real bug.** A user can write the source, or a stream can deliver that chunking, and the codec breaks a rule. Fix the codec, then run `--lower`.
- **Modeling error.** The script compares something the codec does not promise, such as a list id. Fix the script with the reason.
- **Intended change.** A change to what the codec keeps, such as a new syntax it refuses, changes the rule too. Change the rule and `docs/vision/plate.md` together. Never weaken a rule or add a baseline entry to get green.

## Findings

The gate finds 72: 71 `markdown-round-trip` and 1 `markdown-throws`. It found 157 at the sync before this one. Measured on 7bc3175013, the lockfile's move from mdast-util-to-markdown 2.1.2 to 2.2.3 cleared 79 round-trip entries, 42 headings and 37 callouts whose text starts or ends with a break. The CJK plugins' writer cleared 6 more, a break before a bare URL in a paragraph, a quote, a list item or a footnote definition. Read by their examples, the round-trip entries that remain fall into these classes. No log proves that each class has a single cause:

- **An emoji shortcode inside a div.** `<div>:+1:</div>` comes back with the emoji glyph and escaped tags.
- **A bare URL before a shortcode.** `https://a.b:+1:` comes back as text that holds the emoji glyph in place of the shortcode.
- **A bare URL before a trailing break.** `# https://a.b<br/>` serializes the break as `&#xA;`, which the parse again reads into the URL, and so does a callout that holds `https://a.b<br/>`.
- **A break before a bare URL in a callout.** `<callout>\n<br/>https://a.b\n</callout>` serializes the break as a space.
- **An unclosed `<u>`.** The serializer closes it around the text after it, and `<br/><u>` loses its break.
- **A task item that starts with a break.** `- [ ] <br/>` loses its checkbox.
- **Two breaks in a row.** `- <br/><br/>` gains a third newline.

The other entry has one cause:

- **A footnote definition in a quote.** `> [^1]:+1:` throws `Markdown node mapping "blockquote" expected block content.` during serialize. 1 `markdown-throws` entry.

`--inline=3 --part=round-trip` fails 2,496 round trips and 24 throws, and every throw is a footnote definition in a quote. `--blocks=3` fails the gate's 71 round trips and 1 throw, and no streamed source fails. Neither run finds a `markdown-line-endings` case. The deep runs read no baseline.

## What a pass proves

Bugs planted on this tree showed where the gate holds:

- Reverting e1fb01295e's codec changes, which kept links, trailing breaks and table images, gives 330 `markdown-round-trip` and 74 `markdown-throws` findings, 199 of them new. `- a<br/>` drops its break, and a heading with an autolink throws `heading expected inline content`.
- Dropping the `!state.openList` test in `markdownSegments.ts` gives 4 `markdown-stream` and 4 `markdown-stream-final` findings, such as `1. a\n\n1. a` chunked at 1,7,8 and `1. a\na\n\n1. a` chunked at 1,9,10, two lists where one belongs.
- Running the codec of 0cfbdc701a, before the line-ending pass in `markdownConversion.ts` and the bare URL check in `markdownIntrinsics.ts`, gives 405 `markdown-line-endings` findings, such as `- [ ] \n_`, whose CRLF form keeps a leading line ending. It also fails the five bare URL sources that the script held then. `https://a.b.\na` serializes to `https://a.b.\\\na`, which parses again without the link. `<https://a.b'> a` serializes to `https://a.b' a`, which parses again with the `'` cut from the URL.
- Dropping `remarkSeparateEscapes` from the parse in `markdownConversion.ts` gives 7 `markdown-round-trip` findings: the `]`, `www.` and email sources and four div sources, such as `<div>https://a.b </div>`. `<https://a.b]> a` serializes to `https\://a.b] a`, which parses again with a link to `https://a.b`, because mdast-util-gfm-autolink-literal finds the URL in the decoded text.
- Dropping the `MARKDOWN_DEFINITION.test(source)` fallback in `markdownConversion.ts` gives stream findings on `[a]\n\n[a]: b` chunked at 1,11 and `[^1]: a\n\n[^1]: a` chunked at 1,13, a reference that a later definition does not resolve.
- Keeping a segment that ends in an inline node in `markdownConversion.ts` gives a stream finding on `<div>a</div>\n\n<div>a</div>` chunked at 1,18, two text runs that normalization would join.
- Keeping a segment that ends inside an unclosed tag in `markdownConversion.ts` gives 40 `markdown-stream` and 40 `markdown-stream-final` findings, such as `</callout> <callout>\n\n---` chunked at 1,24, a body that falls out of its tag.
- Running the codec of 7bc3175013, before `fix(markdown): round-trip every CommonMark and GFM spec example`, gives 22 new `markdown-round-trip` findings and 1 `markdown-stream` finding, all spec examples. CommonMark example 335 splits its code span into three at its line endings, example 404 closes its emphasis before the link it holds, and example 218 streams `[foo]` as text, though the quote below it defines `foo`.

Each run used the gate bound and restored the product files byte for byte. Each also ran before the lockfile moved to mdast-util-to-markdown 2.2.3, so its round-trip counts are those of the 2.1.2 writer. The 7bc3175013 run used the script of `fix(markdown): round-trip every CommonMark and GFM spec example`, the unclosed tag run the script of `fix(markdown): keep a streamed tag open until its real closer`, and the `remarkSeparateEscapes` run the script of `fix(markdown): read an escaped URL as text`. The `openList` and 0cfbdc701a runs used the script of `fix(markdown): read CR line endings as LF and keep a bare URL a link`, and the others ran at the first sync, `test(platejs): check Markdown round trips and streamed parses on every small document`.

## Coverage record

Synced at `test(markdown): read CJK emphasis before punctuation in the test editor`. The verification describes these files as they stand there:

| File | What the check exercises |
| --- | --- |
| `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/MarkdownPlugin.ts` | `parse`, `parseSlice` and `serialize` |
| `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/internal/markdownConversion.ts` | the full parse, the line-ending pass, checkpoints and segment reuse |
| `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/internal/markdownSegments.ts` | where a streamed parse may commit a segment |
| `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/internal/` | the mappings, tags, lines and intrinsics both directions use |
| `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/deserializer/` | Markdown to nodes |
| `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/serializer/` | nodes to Markdown |
| `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/__tests__/createTestEditor.tsx` | the plugins under test |
| `packages/platejs/src/features/indent/` | the indent plugin the check adds |
| `apps/www/src/registry/components/editor/indent-static.tsx` | the kit's indent targets the check copies |
| `packages/platejs/scripts/markdown-round-trip-gfm.ts` | the GFM examples |
| `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/__tests__/nameListIds.ts` | comparing documents up to list ids |
| `packages/plitejs/scripts/check-harness.ts` | the flags, workers and gate the formal checks share |

To see whether the verification needs another look, find the sync commit with `git log --oneline -1 --grep "read CJK emphasis before punctuation"`, then run `git log --oneline <that commit>..HEAD --` with the table's paths and `packages/platejs/scripts/markdown-round-trip.ts`. For each commit it lists, read the diff and confirm four things. A continuation still reads its previous result only through the checkpoint's segments and the `startsWith` test on its source, so merging previews by segment ends stays sound. New Markdown syntax the codec supports has an inline token or a block form. A new plugin in the test editor that changes what a source parses to is in the gate. The check's indent targets still match the kit's. Move the sync commit in this file forward, naming its subject, only after that check and a run of `pnpm check:markdown-round-trip`.

## What it does not cover

- HTML round trips and the HTML codec.
- Streaming of inline contents at every chunking. The stream part runs only the block sequences, where each form holds `a`, and the `spec-stream` part continues each spec example one character at a time, so other chunkings of inline syntax go unchecked.
- A spec example's expected HTML. The check compares Plate documents, so a parse that differs from the spec's HTML still passes when it round-trips.
- Sources longer than the bound, other than the spec examples, and containers nested inside one another.
- Plugins outside the test editor and the kit's indent, MDX components and custom rules.
- A serialized form that is not the source the user wrote. The check compares documents, not Markdown text.
- What a CJK source parses to. An editor without the CJK plugins reads the CJK sources as text and still round-trips them, so `packages/platejs/src/markdown/lib/cjkEmphasis.spec.ts` asserts their marks and exported bytes.
- A round trip of the two unbalanced tag forms. Their round trips find one bug: under CR-only line endings, `<callout>\r    a` loses its indented text. That bug is in the `markdown` subject's Open work. A round trip of these forms joins the gate once that bug is fixed.
- A second failure on a known source. A case records its first failure per rule, and its identity names only the source, so a known source that starts to fail in a new way, or on another chunking, still matches its baseline entry.
- A parse that refuses a source under the default policy. Such a source counts in `refused` and gets no round trip.
- The editor surface: pasting, AI streaming into a document, and the browser.

`/pstack:maintain-formal-verification-skill` brings this verification back in line after changes that did not recheck it.
